# Kivo

A small, fast and safe desktop cleanup utility built with Python.

Kivo scans selected directories for old temporary files, shows exactly what
it found, calculates the recoverable storage and optionally removes the files.

The project is intentionally minimal. No database, no external dependencies
and no background services.

## Features

- Lightweight
- Zero external dependencies
- Configurable scan locations
- Configurable file extensions
- Minimum file age protection
- Human-readable scan results
- Dry-run mode by default
- Interactive deletion confirmation
- Works with relative or absolute paths
- Simple JSON configuration

## Requirements

Python 3.9+

## Installation

Clone the repository:

    git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/kivo.git

Enter the project:

    cd kivo

No packages are required.

## Configuration

Open `config.json`.

Example:

    {
        "scan_paths": [
            "C:/Users/YourName/Downloads",
            "C:/Users/YourName/AppData/Local/Temp"
        ],
        "extensions": [
            ".tmp",
            ".temp",
            ".log",
            ".bak",
            ".cache"
        ],
        "min_age_days": 7,
        "dry_run": true
    }

Linux example:

    {
        "scan_paths": [
            "/tmp",
            "/home/user/Downloads"
        ]
    }

## Scan

Run:

    python kivo.py --scan

Kivo will inspect the configured directories and display:

- File path
- File size
- File age
- Total recoverable storage

## Cleanup

By default Kivo runs in dry-run mode.

This means no files are deleted.

To enable cleanup, change:

    "dry_run": false

Then run:

    python kivo.py --clean

Kivo will ask for confirmation before deleting anything.

## Safety

Kivo only considers files that:

1. Exist inside configured scan directories.
2. Match one of the configured extensions.
3. Are older than the configured minimum age.

The default configuration uses dry-run mode.

Always review the scan results before enabling deletion.

## Commands

    python kivo.py --scan

Scan configured directories.

    python kivo.py --clean

Scan and optionally remove matching files.

    python kivo.py --version

Show the current Kivo version.

## Project Structure

    kivo/
    ├── README.md
    ├── kivo.py
    ├── config.json
    └── .gitignore

## Why Kivo?

Most cleanup tools try to become huge system utilities.

Kivo takes the opposite approach.

It does one job:

    Find old junk.
    Show it clearly.
    Let you decide.

